    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          GLP-1 medications such as Ozempic, Wegovy, Mounjaro and Zepbound work largely by making you eat less, and that is exactly why protein becomes a problem. When a few bites fill you up, it is easy to go a whole day on crackers, coffee and half a sandwich, and a meaningful share of the weight that comes off can be lean tissue rather than fat. A protein shake will not fix that on its own, but a small, gentle, high-protein one makes it far easier to hit a daily target on a stomach that wants very little. The best protein powder for GLP-1 users packs 20 to 30 g of protein into the smallest, least nauseating serving possible. Here are six that do, and how to use them alongside strength training and your prescriber's advice.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Protein Matters More on a GLP-1 Medication
          </h2>

          <p>
            GLP-1 receptor agonists such as semaglutide, and the dual GIP and GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide, reduce appetite and slow the rate at which the stomach empties. The result is large, sustained weight loss for many people, averaging around 15 percent of body weight with semaglutide and up to about 20 percent with the highest tirzepatide dose in the major trials. The same effect that makes the drugs work also cuts total food intake sharply, and protein tends to fall along with everything else unless you protect it on purpose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Any large weight loss takes some lean mass with it, and the GLP-1 trials are no exception. In a body-composition substudy of the STEP 1 semaglutide trial, lean mass measured by DXA scan made up roughly 40 percent of the weight lost. In the equivalent SURMOUNT-1 substudy for tirzepatide, the figure was roughly a quarter. Those numbers come from small subgroups, the trials measured them differently, and DXA lean mass includes water and organ tissue as well as muscle, so they overstate pure muscle loss. Still, they are a reason to take muscle seriously rather than assume it looks after itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Muscle matters for more than appearance. It supports strength, balance, blood glucose control and resting energy use, and it is harder to regain than fat, particularly after about age 50. Losing a lot of it during weight loss can leave someone lighter but weaker, and it can make weight easier to regain if the medication is stopped. That concern is why nutrition and obesity organizations have put adequate protein and resistance training near the top of their advice for people on these drugs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good news is that the two habits that limit lean mass loss during any diet, eating enough protein and lifting something heavy a few times a week, are simple and well supported by research. A protein shake is not essential, but on days when your appetite is near zero it is often the only practical way to get 25 or 30 g of protein into your body. For the bigger picture on how protein builds and maintains muscle, see our explainer on <a href="/do-protein-supplements-build-muscle/">whether protein supplements build muscle</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">GLP-1 drugs shrink appetite, so protein has to be deliberate.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A meaningful share of the weight lost on these medications can be lean tissue. Enough protein, spread across the day, plus regular strength training is the best-supported way to keep more of it. Small, dense shakes help on days food will not go down.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked: Protein per Ounce, Not per Scoop
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run trials, taste tests or lab analysis for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, published third-party certifications and the research on protein, appetite and lean mass during weight loss, the same method set out on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Comments on taste, texture and tolerance are what buyers widely report, not results of our own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first criterion is protein density by volume, not just by calorie. On a GLP-1 drug the limiting factor is often how much liquid your stomach will accept, so a carton with 30 g of protein in 11 fluid ounces beats one with 20 g in 16 ounces, and a powder that mixes smoothly into four to six ounces of water beats one that needs a full shaker. Every pick delivers at least about 20 g of protein per serving, and most deliver 25 to 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second criterion is gentleness. Fat slows stomach emptying further, which can worsen the fullness and nausea many people already feel, so we favored low-fat products. Lactose can be an extra irritant for some people, so isolates and lactose-free milk shakes scored well. Large amounts of sugar alcohols, which commonly cause bloating and loose stools, counted against a product. Clear, juice-style proteins earned a slot because many people find them easier than a milky shake when nausea is at its worst.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we checked sugar, calories and testing. Most picks have a gram or two of sugar and 100 to 160 calories per serving, which matters because the point is to protect muscle while fat comes off, not to add back calories the medication helped you avoid. We gave credit for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice status where the brand claims it, and we note where we could not confirm a certification.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
