        <div id="protein-targets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Protein to Aim For on Ozempic, Wegovy or Zepbound
          </h2>

          <p>
            The baseline recommended dietary allowance for adults is 0.8 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day. That figure was set to prevent deficiency in healthy people at stable weight, and most experts consider it a floor rather than a target for someone losing weight quickly. During active weight loss, published guidance and expert reviews commonly propose higher intakes, in the range of roughly 1.2 to 1.6 g per kilogram per day, to help limit lean mass loss.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is an important wrinkle. Nobody has settled which body weight to multiply by. For a person carrying a lot of excess weight, using current weight can produce a number that is unrealistically high, which is why some clinicians use an adjusted or reference body weight instead, and why the 2025 joint nutrition advisory on GLP-1 therapy from four US lifestyle-medicine, nutrition and obesity societies stresses individualized targets rather than one formula. Your prescriber or a registered dietitian can give you a specific daily number that accounts for your size, age, activity and kidney function.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, many people find a per-meal goal easier than a daily one. Getting about 20 to 30 g of protein at each of three eating occasions puts most adults in a reasonable range and spreads intake across the day, which also suits the way muscle uses protein. On a GLP-1 drug, that might mean a Greek yogurt at breakfast, a shake mid-morning or after a workout, and a palm-sized portion of fish, chicken, tofu or eggs eaten first at dinner. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> and guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> show the arithmetic.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have chronic kidney disease, these higher targets may not apply. Guidance for people with non-dialysis kidney disease generally points to about 0.8 g per kilogram per day, so get an individual number before adding shakes. The same applies if you have liver disease or any condition where a doctor has told you to limit protein.
          </p>

