            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting protein fall with appetite</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When you can only manage a few bites, it is easy to fill them with toast, crackers or coffee. Eat protein first at every meal, then add a small shake if you are well short of your target. Without that, lean mass loss tends to be larger than it needs to be.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forcing down a full, thick shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 16-ounce creamy shake on a slow stomach is a recipe for nausea. Sip half a bottle, mix powder into four to six ounces of liquid, or switch to a cold clear whey. Small, frequent servings beat one large one when your stomach is the limit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on protein without strength training</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein supplies the building blocks, but resistance exercise tells the body to keep muscle. Two or three short strength sessions a week, even with bands or bodyweight, make your protein work much harder. Ask a physical therapist for a plan if you have joint or mobility issues.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring fluids and fiber</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Shakes add almost no fiber, and reduced thirst plus side effects can leave you dehydrated and constipated. Sip water through the day, add berries, oats, beans or chia where you can, and call your prescriber if vomiting or diarrhea stops you keeping fluids down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a weight-loss protein target with kidney disease</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Higher protein targets proposed for active weight loss may not suit people with chronic kidney disease, who are generally advised to stay near 0.8 g per kilogram. Get an individual number from your doctor or dietitian before adding 50 g or more a day from shakes.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
