        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best protein powder for GLP-1 users?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for at least 20 to 25 g of protein in a small, low-fat serving. A dense whey isolate such as Dymatize ISO100 mixes into a few ounces of water, clear whey is easiest on nausea, and Fairlife Nutrition Plan packs about 30 g into an 11.5 ounce bottle. The best one is the one your stomach accepts consistently.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I drink protein shakes on Ozempic or Wegovy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are not required, but they help many people. Appetite drops sharply on semaglutide, and shakes make it easier to reach a daily protein target when solid food will not go down. Eat protein-rich foods first where possible and use one or two small shakes to fill the gap, alongside regular strength training.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein do I need on a GLP-1 medication?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The adult RDA is 0.8 g per kilogram, but proposed targets during active weight loss commonly sit around 1.2 to 1.6 g per kilogram, with debate about which body weight to use. A practical goal is 20 to 30 g at each meal. Your prescriber or dietitian can set an individual number, especially if you have kidney disease.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do GLP-1 drugs cause muscle loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Like any large weight loss, they lead to some loss of lean mass. Body-composition substudies of the major semaglutide and tirzepatide trials found lean mass made up roughly a quarter to two-fifths of weight lost, though those scans also count water and organ tissue. Enough protein plus resistance training is the best-supported way to limit it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What protein shake is best when I feel nauseous on Mounjaro or Zepbound?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A clear whey isolate mixed with cold water is often easiest, because it is thin, nearly fat-free and tastes more like a sports drink. Sip slowly and stop when full. If nausea comes with persistent vomiting, severe abdominal pain or signs of dehydration, contact your prescriber rather than pushing through.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can protein shakes replace meals on a GLP-1 drug?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Occasionally, on days food will not go down, but they should not become your main diet. Whole foods supply fiber, vitamins and minerals that most shakes lack, and nutrient intake can fall short when total food volume is very low. If you are relying on shakes most days, ask your care team or a dietitian for help.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are protein shakes safe with GLP-1 and diabetes medication?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes, but combining a GLP-1 drug with insulin or a sulfonylurea raises the risk of low blood glucose, and replacing meals with low-carbohydrate shakes can raise it further. Your prescriber may need to adjust doses. Treat lows with fast-acting carbohydrate rather than protein, and check before changing your eating pattern significantly.
              </p>
